Find one change to the portable readme that helps a stranger carry out the
user's intent. Keeping the original is a valid outcome. Save a run as an
ordinary game with a frozen copy of the instructions that govern the trial.

Give fresh agents the same starting product. Ask one to save a game where
two inspectors submit proposals and a third picks one. Ask another to save
a game where three players owe exactly one recommendation between them and
own the choice of how to get there. Ask a third to discuss the arrangement
before saving anything. Have someone else reserve one more request and its
observable success condition before the editor sees it.

For the public entry point, give a stranger in an unrelated repository just
the source repository URL and a loosely worded request to fix something
there. Compare with the same request to discuss it first. Inspect the original
working tree and see whether a new participant can recover the outcome.
Leave a stopped sibling task in place. Check that the new task can proceed,
that the sibling stays intact, and that existing Git ignore settings survive.

Inspect the files for an actual mismatch. Try the smallest edit that could
fix it. Compare both readmes with the same requests, product, model, tools
and turn limit in fresh directories. Repeat the decisive case, try another
model, and open the reserved request after the candidate is frozen.

Authorize a short game in a copy. At a decision, fork its files and drop the
chat. Give a new player the next turn in each copy; put a nested stop file
in one first. Check what each player actually did. Keep trial trees and any
copied stop markers outside the evaluating game.

Give a fresh reader anonymized results and the request. Verify its claims
against files and working outputs. Record which decisions changed, what
still failed and why the edit should be kept or dropped. Finish after one
candidate. A further idea can be tried in another game.
